title: Knowledge Graphs as Epistemic Devices: The Experiments in Art and Technology Case Study
---
Cristina Pattuelli, Matt Miller, and Ava Kaplan have published an article in Springer’s Archival Science journal, "Knowledge Graphs as Epistemic Devices: The Experiments in Art and Technology Case Study" Archival Science, 26(46). [doi.org/10.1007/s10502-026-09567-4](doi.org/10.1007/s10502-026-09567-4) Preprint: Research Square, [https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9603047/v1](https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9603047/v1).
This case study uses Semantic Lab’s E.A.T. Knowledge graph project to demonstrate how semantic technologies can make complex, distributed cultural practices computationally tractable while preserving their experimental and relational character. 
